    ```
A: The problem is located within PUT_ClearContract() method, where it performs the arrList.Clear() to clear out the original list. We then attempt to observe all the elements in this list using a foreach loop, this is where it fails if the list is empty after being cleared.

Here, we don't want to execute this part of the testing (assuming Understand under test), so we have added following code:
```csharp
AssumePrecondition.IsFalse(arrList.Count == 0);
```
Thus, when the arrList is cleared, the test case will be skipped since we don't have a PreContract that would allow arrList to be empty.

I hope this solution fits your needs.
Please provide additional feedback for more specific solution.
